WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.690
Shadowing, uh, of the Mini Stories,
which we have in all of our

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:07.470
languages, is something that I'm
going to do more of in the new year.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:14.100
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here and today,
I want to talk about goals for 2022,

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.390
but not in terms of my language goals,
but in terms of my activity goals.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:22.680
And I mentioned that in the video that
I did earlier on my language goals.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:26.760
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:27.720
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:33.480
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a review.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:38.700
So I told you that I was going to
continue focusing on Arabic and Persian.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:43.940
Uh, I might dabble a bit in Turkish, but
if we do launch Punjabi or some other

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:48.960
other South, or at least Indian, uh,
languages at LingQ, I will try to get

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.559
a bit of a sense of that language as
well as Tagalog, because we have a lot

00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:57.480
of Punjabi speakers and Filipinos in
Vancouver, whether they speak, whether

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.050
they're native speakers of Tagalog or
not, I don't know, but I think most people

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:02.809
in the Philippines do speak Tagalog.

00:01:03.629 --> 00:01:06.360
So I'll get my jollies
surprising them, say.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.070
So those are language goals.

00:01:08.119 --> 00:01:12.060
Now activity goals, uh, one of the
things that I want to do in the

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:13.710
new year is I want to read more.

00:01:14.645 --> 00:01:20.945
I look back to my sort of Chinese
learning, uh, experience and, uh, I

00:01:20.945 --> 00:01:24.305
did a lot of reading, more reading
than I'm doing now in my Arabic and

00:01:24.305 --> 00:01:26.525
Persian because I had less audio.

00:01:26.705 --> 00:01:29.345
So now I can sort of
say, okay, I'm listening.

00:01:29.345 --> 00:01:30.155
I listened to something.

00:01:30.155 --> 00:01:30.695
I picked it up.

00:01:30.695 --> 00:01:33.545
I listened to half an hour while
preparing breakfast and cleaning

00:01:33.545 --> 00:01:34.875
up or I listened to it in the car.

00:01:35.075 --> 00:01:36.105
So I've done my thing.

00:01:36.735 --> 00:01:39.495
Uh, actually, when I was
learning Chinese, I read a lot.

00:01:39.585 --> 00:01:41.835
I read a lot and reading is powerful.

00:01:42.015 --> 00:01:45.625
So I've said in the new year,
I'm going to read more and I can

00:01:45.625 --> 00:01:48.179
read at LingQ in sentence mode.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.420
And I can hear each sentence if
it's timestamped or I can use the

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:56.190
five-second rewind to, uh, to make sure
I've got the audio for that sentence.

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:58.920
But to some extent I'm cheating
because I'm listening to it

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.780
at the same time and it...

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:03.810
reading in, uh, Arabic
and Persian is difficult.

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:08.640
But I'm going to force myself to do
more reading where I don't listen so

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.020
that I'm forcing myself to try to rely
on my ability to read the language.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:17.340
So that's one of my goals, do more
reading and I definitely would recommend

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.830
people that don't neglect your reading.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:23.400
Reading is a powerful way for your
brain to get used to the language.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:25.110
That's number one.

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:28.140
Number two, I am going
to do more shadowing.

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:29.190
I've started...

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.800
I mentioned shadowing in a previous video.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:37.890
I've never been a big fan of shadowing uh,
partly because a lot of my listening is

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.030
listening to things, you know, whether it
be politics or economics, things where I'm

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:45.839
trying to understand what they're saying,
and if I shadow, then it confuses me and

00:02:45.839 --> 00:02:47.259
I can't really hear what their saying.

00:02:48.119 --> 00:02:52.830
But what I have discovered is that
our Mini Stories are ideal for

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:55.110
shadowing because inevitably...

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.660
shadowing by the way, is when
you listen to something and you

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:59.310
try to speak at the same time.

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.310
It's not a pause where
you repeat what you heard.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:06.780
It's actually trying to imitate
what you hear simultaneously.

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:11.400
And I just find that that's, if I'm
listening to something that I'm genuinely

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.150
interested in, I find that very difficult
to do, and I quickly stop doing it.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:18.255
But I said to myself, I'm going
to give this another chance.

00:03:18.255 --> 00:03:22.035
So I tried it with different kinds
of material, and I find that with

00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:26.234
the Mini Stories, it's great because
the Mini Stories are simpler.

00:03:26.685 --> 00:03:30.734
I know the stories, there's so
much repetition that if I miss a

00:03:30.734 --> 00:03:35.445
word in the first point of view of
the story, I'll pick it up in the

00:03:35.445 --> 00:03:39.015
second point of view or I'll pick
it up in the questions and answers.

00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:42.915
So it lends itself to a shadowing.

00:03:43.334 --> 00:03:47.535
Second of all, I find that as I listen to
these mini stories, which I've listened

00:03:47.535 --> 00:03:50.355
to many times, sometimes I lose focus.

00:03:50.984 --> 00:03:54.015
And so even if I listen to it another
time and another time, I'm not

00:03:54.045 --> 00:03:58.015
gaining anything because I'm kind
of skipping over the same parts that

00:03:58.015 --> 00:03:59.325
I...that I skipped over the last time.

00:03:59.565 --> 00:04:04.214
Now I'm forcing myself to actually
say what I hear and it forces me

00:04:04.214 --> 00:04:07.665
to focus in, and I notice things
that I didn't notice before.

00:04:08.084 --> 00:04:09.855
And so, and I enjoy it now.

00:04:09.855 --> 00:04:13.995
So now for example, I was just on my
nordic track, you know, cross country

00:04:13.995 --> 00:04:15.975
ski exercising thing that I have at home.

00:04:16.515 --> 00:04:22.275
And I was listening to my mini stories in
Levantine Arabic, and I enjoy doing it.

00:04:22.275 --> 00:04:23.055
It's a challenge.

00:04:23.085 --> 00:04:26.865
It just, it's a challenge to try to
shadow with what you're listening to.

00:04:27.465 --> 00:04:30.705
Uh, there's words there that
I noticed that I don't know.

00:04:30.705 --> 00:04:33.465
So now I'm motivated to go and
read them again in the story.

00:04:33.855 --> 00:04:39.030
And so I think that the shadowing of
the Mini Stories uh, which we have in

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:43.170
all of our languages, is something that
I'm going to do more of in the new year.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:48.690
So more reading, more shadowing
and perhaps a little more

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:50.370
flashcarding of phrases.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:54.720
I, I'm not convinced that flashcarding
words is all that useful, but with

00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:00.180
phrases, key phrases to focus in on
those phrases that will help me speak

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:02.760
Arabic and Persian a little bit better.

00:05:03.645 --> 00:05:08.715
So there, you have it, a bit of a, of a
sense of, um, some of my, uh, language

00:05:08.745 --> 00:05:10.365
activity goals for the new year.

00:05:10.905 --> 00:05:13.605
And I'll leave you the video
that I did on shadowing.

00:05:14.025 --> 00:05:15.915
And, uh, also another one.

00:05:15.945 --> 00:05:18.435
Uh, the one I did about my language goals.

00:05:18.645 --> 00:05:19.425
Thank you for listening.

